course: dictionary
lesson: 3331
title: Every: each one, every time
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Every means each one of a group, with none left out. «Every one of these machines swaps force for distance or speed» means all of them, not just some. Try it: if you can put «each» in its place and the sentence still makes sense, every is doing its job.
explain: With a time word, every means again and again: «every day», «every week», «every winter». «Every other day» is different: it means one, skip one, the next. So every other day means Monday, Wednesday, Friday, not every single day.
explain: With a number, every shows how often something happens: «every 10 minutes» means a repeat at a fixed gap, like the bell at 9:00, 9:10, 9:20. Every is always followed by one singular noun: every child, every month, never every children.
fact: Every means each one of a group, with none left out.
fact: «Every day» and «every time» mean again and again.
fact: «Every other» means one, skip one, the next: every other day.
fact: «Every 10 minutes» means a repeat at a fixed gap of 10 minutes.
fact: Casual talk: «every man and his dog» (UK, Australia) means a great many people. Formal writing says «each and every», «at intervals of», «on each occasion».
exercise: «Every one of these machines swaps force for distance or speed.» What does every one mean here? | Most of them, but not all | *Each one, with none left out | Only some of them | Just the first one
exercise: «Fixed costs are bills that come every month, like rent.» How often do bills like rent come? | *Again and again, once each month | Once a year, in winter | Only once in a whole life | Only when you have spare money
exercise: Jayden trains every other day. Which days does he train in a week? | Saturday and Sunday only | Every day, without a break | *Monday, Wednesday, Friday | Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday
test: «Grown-ups fall for these every day, which is exactly why the tricks are used.» What does every day mean? | On one special day only | Only on Sundays | *Each day, again and again | Once, long ago
test: The bell rings «every 10 minutes» during the test. How often does it ring? | Once in total | *Once at each gap of 10 minutes | Never before noon | Only at lunchtime
exam: The report says the readings were taken «at intervals of» ten minutes, «on each occasion» with the same care. What does «at intervals of» mean? | *At regular gaps of | In a loud voice | Without any reason at all | At the very end only
